Dual-use disclosure for mcp-icloud
===================================

mcp-icloud is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that lets an AI assistant
(e.g. Claude Desktop) operate the USER'S OWN Apple services on the USER'S OWN
machine, at the user's request. It is the Apple-services equivalent of the
official MCP reference servers (filesystem, github, etc.).

Why this package can look like malware to automated scanning
------------------------------------------------------------
- It reads and sends email, reads contacts, calendars, reminders, notes and
  iMessage conversations, and controls Safari — via AppleScript (local mode)
  or the user's iCloud account over IMAP/CalDAV/CardDAV (cloud mode).
- It executes osascript as a child process to drive macOS applications.
- Cloud mode authenticates with an app-specific password supplied by the user.

Why this is legitimate dual-use functionality
---------------------------------------------
- The server only runs when the user installs and starts it, and only talks
  over stdio to the MCP client the user connected it to. There is no network
  listener, no telemetry, no analytics, and no data leaves the machine except
  toward Apple's own servers (iCloud) on the user's behalf.
- On macOS, every application the server touches triggers the operating
  system's TCC consent prompts (System Settings > Privacy & Security >
  Automation); nothing is accessible until the user grants it.
- Credentials are supplied by the user via a local .env file or the MCP
  bundle's OS credential store, are read once at startup, and are never
  logged. Every tool input is schema-validated (zod) before execution, and
  values interpolated into AppleScript are coerced and escaped.
- Full source: https://github.com/MrGo2/icloud-mcp (MIT license, tests and
  CI public).

Intended users: individuals connecting an AI assistant to their own Apple
account and macOS applications. The package provides no capability against
third parties that the operating system's own consent model does not gate.
